#: ../../../dev/patterns_to_avoid.md:3
msgid "Common Mistakes and Legacy Patterns"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/patterns_to_avoid.md:5
msgid ""
"LLVM-libc's conventions have changed over the years, and the tree still "
"contains code written to older ones. **Existing code is not precedent.** A "
"pattern being present in the tree, even many times, does not make it "
"acceptable in a new change. This page lists the mistakes reviewers see most "
"often and the old patterns that should not be copied."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/patterns_to_avoid.md:11
msgid ""
"This applies with particular force to changes written with AI assistance, "
"because a tool that imitates nearby code will imitate its outdated parts too."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/patterns_to_avoid.md:14
msgid ""
"Larger areas of the tree that are known to need rework are listed in {ref}"
"`roadmap`. Do not model new code on them. Issues for LLVM-libc are filed "
"under the `libc` label in the [issue tracker](https://github.com/llvm/llvm-"
"project/labels/libc)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/patterns_to_avoid.md:19
msgid "Most common mistakes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/patterns_to_avoid.md:21
msgid ""
"**Calling one entrypoint from another.** It is the natural way to write many "
"functions and it is not allowed. Some existing code does it; that code is to "
"be cleaned up and is not an example to follow. See {ref}"
"`entrypoints_do_not_call_entrypoints`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/patterns_to_avoid.md:25
msgid ""
"**Mimicking another libc's interface.** Changing a type's layout, a "
"function's observable behavior, or the set of exported symbols solely to "
"match another libc is sometimes the quickest way to get an existing program "
"running. That matching is not the intent for LLVM-libc, and goes against its "
"design principles. See: {ref}`making_existing_software_work`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/patterns_to_avoid.md:30
msgid "**Copying an outdated pattern from old code.** See the table below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/patterns_to_avoid.md:31
msgid ""
"**Copying or translating code from another libc.** Neither is allowed. {ref}"
"`original_implementations` states where the line is between learning from "
"another implementation and taking from it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/patterns_to_avoid.md:34
msgid ""
"**Leaving out part of the registration.** A new function needs an entry in "
"`libc/include/<header>.yaml` and in the relevant `entrypoints.txt` files in "
"addition to its implementation. A new public type needs its own registration "
"as well. See {ref}`implementing_a_function`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/patterns_to_avoid.md:39
msgid "Legacy patterns"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/patterns_to_avoid.md:0
msgid "Seen in the tree"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/patterns_to_avoid.md:0
msgid "Write this instead"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/patterns_to_avoid.md:0
msgid ""
"A first line of the form `//===-- Title ---...---===//` in a file header"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/patterns_to_avoid.md:0
msgid ""
"The current LLVM file header, shown in {ref}`implementation_guidelines`. "
"Update the header of any file you touch."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/patterns_to_avoid.md:0
msgid ""
"`syscall_impl(...)` in an entrypoint, followed by a hand-written check of "
"the result"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/patterns_to_avoid.md:0
msgid ""
"A wrapper in `src/__support/OSUtil/<os>/syscall_wrappers/` that uses "
"`syscall_checked`. See {ref}`syscall_wrapper_refactor`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/patterns_to_avoid.md:0
msgid "Functions and variables inside an anonymous namespace"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/patterns_to_avoid.md:0
msgid ""
"`static` functions and variables. Anonymous namespaces are for types only, "
"as in the [LLVM coding standards](https://llvm.org/docs/"
"CodingStandards.html#restrict-visibility)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/patterns_to_avoid.md:0
msgid ""
"A `.cpp` file that gets its types from the implementation header it includes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/patterns_to_avoid.md:0
msgid ""
"Include what you use: each file includes the proxy header for every type and "
"macro it names."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/patterns_to_avoid.md:0
msgid "`#include <pthread.h>` or another public header in `libc/src/`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/patterns_to_avoid.md:0
msgid "The proxy headers in `libc/hdr/`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/patterns_to_avoid.md:0
msgid "`libc_errno` assigned inside an internal helper"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/patterns_to_avoid.md:0
msgid ""
"The helper returns `ErrorOr<T>` and the entrypoint assigns `libc_errno`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/patterns_to_avoid.md:0
msgid "A new file or directory named `utils`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/patterns_to_avoid.md:0
msgid "A name that says what the code does."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/patterns_to_avoid.md:0
msgid "`ASSERT_TRUE(a == b)` and `ASSERT_TRUE(p != nullptr)` in tests"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/patterns_to_avoid.md:0
msgid ""
"`ASSERT_EQ(a, b)` and `ASSERT_NE(p, nullptr)`, which print both values on "
"failure."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/patterns_to_avoid.md:0
msgid ""
"A bare path literal such as `\"testdata/file.txt\"` or `\"/nonexistent\"` in "
"tests"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/patterns_to_avoid.md:0
msgid ""
"`libc_make_test_file_path(...)`, including for paths that are meant not to "
"exist."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/patterns_to_avoid.md:53
msgid "Code that is hard to review"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/patterns_to_avoid.md:55
msgid ""
"These are not style violations in the formatting sense. They make code hard "
"to reason about, and reviewers will ask for them to be rewritten."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/patterns_to_avoid.md:58
msgid ""
"**Deeply nested control flow.** An `if` inside a loop inside a loop inside "
"an `if` is difficult to follow. Collapse conditions, return early, and move "
"inner blocks into helper functions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/patterns_to_avoid.md:61
msgid ""
"**`while (true)` where the exit test is the first or last statement of the "
"body.** That test is the loop condition. Write it as a `while` or `do`/"
"`while` condition. The same applies to a body that ends in `if (x) continue; "
"else return;`. A loop whose exit test is in the middle of the body is a "
"different case, and `while (true)` with a `break` is a reasonable way to "
"write it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/patterns_to_avoid.md:67
msgid ""
"**Lambdas used as local helper functions.** A lambda that captures local "
"variables has inputs that do not appear at the call site, so the call no "
"longer shows what the helper reads or changes. If the code is only being "
"factored out for reuse or readability, write a `static` function and pass "
"what it needs explicitly. A short lambda that is passed to another function "
"as a callable is fine."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/patterns_to_avoid.md:73
msgid ""
"**Templates that accept more than the code supports.** A function template "
"whose body only works for subclasses of one class, or for callables with one "
"signature, should say so in its declaration, so that the compiler rejects an "
"argument that makes no sense. Take the base class by reference, or take a "
"`cpp::function_ref` with the expected signature, instead of an unconstrained "
"`typename T`. Where a template is needed, constrain it, for example with "
"`cpp::enable_if_t` or a `static_assert`. Overloading a function for the "
"specific types it supports is fine, and so is a template that is generic on "
"purpose. The same applies to variadic argument forwarding."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/patterns_to_avoid.md:82
msgid ""
"**Magic constants.** A bare `7`, `61`, or `0xFFF` in an expression leaves "
"the reader to work out what it stands for. Give every such constant a name "
"that says what it means. Multiplying by `DAYS_PER_WEEK` explains itself; "
"multiplying by `7` does not."
msgstr ""
